---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';
import CapStrip from '../components/CapStrip.astro';
import Ledger from '../components/Ledger.astro';
import SystemPlate from '../components/SystemPlate.astro';
import OverviewPlate from '../components/OverviewPlate.astro';
import PrivateFigure from '../components/figures/PrivateFigure.astro';
import { projects } from '../data/projects.js';
import { privateSystems } from '../data/private-systems.js';
import '../styles/systems.css';
---

<Layout>
  <!-- The hero — the sitter's record -->
  <Plate
    id="top"
    class="hero"
  >
    <div class="plate-grid">
      <div class="plate-copy">
        {/* D23, finally shipped. This read "AI that shows its work" — the
            truncation D23 decided against on 08 Aug in as many words: "the
            current headline truncates it and claims half."

            Worse, D40 then TESTED it, kept it, and wrote down exactly why the
            shipped half is the weak one: "shows its work" partially fails the
            table-stake red flag because every RAG product claims citations
            now, while "refuse to fake it" is the half nobody else claims.
            The site was shipping the failing half and accenting SHOWS, its
            weakest word.

            Two recorded decisions, unshipped, and a planning document that
            said it had applied them. Caught by /impeccable critique, not by me.

            The accent moves to `refuse`, which is the differentiator. */}
        <h1>AI systems that show their work and <span class="accent">refuse</span> to fake it</h1>
        {/* Phase 0 applied — docs/positioning-phase0.md.
            The lede used to read "…fourteen years across distributed systems and
            production reliability, NOW building independent AI systems". Read
            cold that says career changer, eight months in — the weakest reading
            of his strongest fact. The fourteen years are now the setup for the
            punchline rather than the thing before it. Same facts, no new claim.

            D27 — the avatar sits beside the name, not above the headline. The
            hero plate was already 191px over a 900px viewport, and this is the
            only placement that fits without dropping the proof card. */}
        <p class="lede">
          <img
            class="avatar"
            src="/rohit-agrawal.webp"
            width="176"
            height="176"
            alt=""
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <strong class="lede-name">Rohit Agrawal</strong> — principal engineer. From
          understanding the requirement to what's actually running in production — and feeding
          what I learn back into the next release. Fourteen years deciding whether software was
          safe to release; now I build AI systems that make that decision about themselves.
        </p>
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#systems">What I built</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="/cv">CV</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="mailto:rohit.ra.agrawal@gmail.com">Email me</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      {/* The ledger is a sibling of the copy, not a child, so that on a phone
          the artefact can sit between the buttons and the career facts. The
          proof of the headline should be seen before the CV detail, and it
          used to be twelve screens down. */}
      {/* Phase 0, item 0.5 cut `Seeking` from FIVE job titles to one, because a
          five-item list reads as "does not know what he wants" in a thirty-second
          skim. That comment already named the opposite failure: a title too
          narrow to search reads as "not my req", and is just as expensive.

          P-3, resolved 2026-08-12 by the owner. The cut went too far — it took the
          quality half of his record off the hero entirely, which is the half that
          makes him unusual. Restored as TWO GROUPED HALVES rather than a list of
          five: the grouping is what stops it reading as scattered, and it now says
          the same three AI-platform names the CONTACT plate had been saying alone,
          twelve screens further down. The hero and the contact plate disagreed
          until now, and the hero is the one a recruiter actually reads.

          Deliberately NOT included: SDET and QA Engineer. Both read a level below
          Senior/Principal and would confuse which level he wants. The fourteen
          years of quality work is the CREDENTIAL, not the target, and it is stated
          as such on both surfaces.

          DEF-29 closed here too. `Base — Bengaluru, India · IST (UTC+5:30)` was
          stated verbatim in this ledger AND in the contact ledger. Same defect
          class as DEF-27, same resolution: the row carrying strictly more
          survives, and the contact ledger also states international travel. The
          `Relocation` row goes with it — DEF-27's fix had REGRESSED, and both
          ledgers were stating relocation again.

          `Led` is new. D32 measured that "led and mentored 11+ engineers"
          appeared ZERO times on the live site, and for senior and principal
          roles scope of responsibility is often the first filter. */}
      <div class="hero-ledger">
        <Ledger
          label="The record"
          rows={[
            { t: 'Experience', d: '14+ yrs — Oracle, Amazon, LimeRoad, Mobileum, Snapdeal, Subex' },
            { t: 'Most recent', d: 'Oracle Principal MTS · 2019–2026' },
            { t: 'Since April 2026', d: 'Building independent AI systems, full time' },
            { t: 'Led', d: '11+ quality engineers and SDETs, globally' },
            {
              t: 'Seeking',
              d:
                'Senior / Principal — AI platform engineering, LLMOps, forward-deployed AI · ' +
                'AI quality engineering, test automation and agentic AI',
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
      {/* O-HERO resolved. The refusal card that used to lead the hero moved
          to the CiteVyn plate below (real data, only its position moved) —
          out of context "REFUSED" reads as broken before a visitor learns
          it's a deliberate refusal. Replaced with the pull-quote (fills the
          whitespace freed by the label removal, item 3) and a proof strip
          of real counted figures — the first thing read is evidence, not
          a "no." */}
      <div class="plate-figure">
        <p class="hero-quote">
          "If your AI features work in the demo and not in the release, we should talk."
        </p>
        <CapStrip
          label="The record, in numbers"
          items={[
            /* Owner 08-14: the bare fraction read as shortfall. A recruiter
               lens diagnosed the symptom (D79 records its deletion fix as
               rejected); the owner's fix is completion — all three facts. */
            { t: 'Total systems', d: '6' },
            { t: 'Built', d: '4' },
            { t: 'In progress', d: '2' },
            { t: 'Release gates', d: '4' },
            { t: 'Golden cases passed', d: '52 / 52' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Plate>

  {/* The overview index, then the four systems, one plate each.
      D76 removed the old #overview for restating four plates at length; the
      owner found the loss of the six-system view was itself a defect, and
      package B restored it as an INDEX — rows, not cards (see OverviewPlate).
      It sits INSIDE #systems as the first child so the nav's plural `Systems`
      and the hero CTA land on an index of six, not past it — DEF-34 is a
      plural label landing on ONE system, and an index is the opposite case.
      Each plate keeps its own painted ground, overview included — palette.css
      exists to make scrolling change the light. */}
  <section id="systems">
    <OverviewPlate />
    {Object.keys(projects).map((slug) => <SystemPlate slug={slug} />)}
  </section>

  <!-- Plate Nº 06 — the private work, on unpainted linen -->
  <Plate
    id="private"
    state="In progress — closed"
    theme="light"
  >
    <div class="plate-grid">
      <div class="plate-copy">
        <h2 class="plate-title tight">Carried, not shown</h2>
        <p>
          Two systems are still being built. They stay closed until they can be judged on
          finished work rather than on intent. They are named because a record that discloses
          its gaps should also say what exists — and the question each one is built to answer
          costs nothing to state.
        </p>
        {/* One source with the overview rows — private-systems.js. */}
        <div class="private-pair">
          {Object.values(privateSystems).map((s) => (
            <div class="private-card">
              <h3>{s.name}</h3>
              <p class="q">{s.q}</p>
              <p>{s.desc}</p>
              <span class="seal">{s.state}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="plate-figure">
        <PrivateFigure />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Plate>

  <!-- Plate Nº 07 — contact -->
  <Plate
    id="contact"
    class="contact"
  >
    <div class="plate-grid">
      <div class="plate-copy">
        <h2 class="plate-title">One message away</h2>
        {/* The demo/release line — positioning-phase0.md §04's sharpest
            asset — now opens the HERO (item 1), not this plate; stating it
            twice is the duplicate-statement pattern DEF-27/DEF-29 already
            fixed. The roles-sought line, already here, is promoted to open
            the plate on its own. */}
        <p>
          Open to senior and principal roles in AI platform engineering, LLMOps and
          forward-deployed AI — and to AI quality engineering, test automation and agentic AI
          leadership. Fourteen years of evaluation and release governance sits underneath all
          of them.
        </p>
        {/* CV was absent here. A recruiter reaching this plate has already
            decided to act, and on a phone the nav shows nothing (DEF-42), so
            this was the end of the funnel with no route to the CV at all. */}
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn primary" href="mailto:rohit.ra.agrawal@gmail.com">Email me</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="/cv">CV</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/rohitagrawal14" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/imrohitagrawal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
        </div>
        {/* The three destinations above are already labelled links. Repeating them
            here as plain text was a duplicate a visitor could not click. These
            rows carry facts the paragraph was burying instead. Terms stay sentence
            case — global.css applies text-transform: uppercase.

            D42 — the H-1B. The owner's instruction: state it, but do not let the
            page read as though he is only looking at the United States.

            The fix is FRAMING, not wording. "H-1B approved" on its own reads as a
            destination. `Work authorisation` reads as paperwork — a list of
            places where the question is already settled. So relocation worldwide
            is stated FIRST and stays the headline fact; the authorisation row
            follows it and names two countries rather than one. A US recruiter
            gets the answer to "will I have to sponsor and wait" without any
            other reader being told the search is American.

            It also fits the voice rule exactly. This is not his urgency and it
            is not a pitch — it is the removal of an objection the READER has,
            which is the distinction the owner drew on 08 Aug and which
            AGENTS.md now records. */}
        <Ledger
          label="Where"
          rows={[
            { t: 'Based', d: 'Bengaluru, India · IST (UTC+5:30)' },
            { t: 'Open to', d: 'Relocation worldwide · international travel' },
            { t: 'Work authorisation', d: 'India · United States — H-1B approved' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Plate>
</Layout>
